  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "⚔️ Redguard Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for the warriors and sailors of Hammerfell: one strong name, a family name with al-, at- or af-, or an epithet."
    },
    "intro": "Most Redguards in Skyrim go by a single name: Nazeem, who never lets you forget he's been to the Cloud District; Saadia at the Bannered Mare; Kematu, who comes looking for her. This **Redguard name generator** makes new names in that style, and the family names with al-, at- and af- you meet in Elder Scrolls Online, for Skyrim, ESO or your own story. It's a fan-made, unofficial tool, not connected to Bethesda Softworks or ZeniMax, and it never gives you the name of a character from the games.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How Redguard names work",
        "content": "<p>Redguard names in the games come in three shapes:</p><ul><li><strong>One name.</strong> This is the usual Skyrim pattern: <strong>Nazeem</strong> and <strong>Amren</strong> in Whiterun, <strong>Saadia</strong> and <strong>Kematu</strong>, whose feud you can settle either way.</li><li><strong>A family name with a particle.</strong> Elder Scrolls Online gives many Redguards a family name joined by a short lowercase particle: <strong>Kamiran at-Kalan</strong> in Sentinel, <strong>Khosren al-Bergama</strong>. Skyrim has one too, in <strong>Lu'ah Al-Skaven</strong>. Lore writers and fans read <em>al-</em> as 'from', followed by a home city, so Khosren al-Bergama is Khosren from Bergama. What <em>at-</em> and <em>af-</em> mean exactly isn't settled, but both tie a person to their family.</li><li><strong>An epithet.</strong> A few Redguards are known by a description: <strong>Majid the Mad</strong> and <strong>Rochelle the Red</strong> in Skyrim.</li></ul><p>The generator's <em>Family name</em> format follows the ESO pattern: <em>at-</em> and <em>af-</em> come before a new family name, and <em>al-</em> before a real city of Hammerfell such as Taneth, Rihad or Gilane.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Eight good Redguard names",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Hadira</strong>: a single name with the soft, open sound of Saadia.</li><li><strong>Korrazim</strong>: double consonants and a strong ending, for a warrior.</li><li><strong>Basim al-Taneth</strong>: from Taneth, a city in the south of Hammerfell.</li><li><strong>Mehrnaz at-Suhrad</strong>: a family name for a merchant house.</li><li><strong>Fazrim al-Elinhir</strong>: from Elinhir, the city in Craglorn.</li><li><strong>Kirzan the Hawk</strong>: an epithet for a scout or an archer.</li><li><strong>Makali the Sword-Dancer</strong>: Redguards are famous as Tamriel's most gifted warriors, and some of them know it.</li><li><strong>Zabbadar the Stargazer</strong>: a navigator, or a dreamer.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Yokuda, Hammerfell and the sword",
        "content": "<p>The Redguards' ancestors came to Tamriel from Yokuda, a lost continent to the west, and took Hammerfell for themselves. Their warrior culture came with them, including the sword-singers of old Yokuda, and it's why Redguards in every game are known for blades.</p><p>Hammerfell's politics give a character a ready-made background. The <strong>Crowns</strong> are traditionalists who trace their line to Yokuda's nobility; the <strong>Forebears</strong> descend from the warriors who first conquered the land and are readier to deal with the Empire. A Crown might insist on a family name; a Forebear sailor might never use one.</p><p>Many of the names in the games sound Arabic or Persian, and so do the names here. Redguards are a fictional people, though, so this page doesn't give the names meanings: very little of the Yokudan language is known, and the names are made for Tamriel, not translated.</p><p>For the Redguards' northern neighbours, try the <a href=\"breton-name-generator.html\">Breton Name Generator</a>; for the Empire they've fought and served, the <a href=\"imperial-name-generator.html\">Imperial Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Do Redguards have last names?",
        "answer": "In Skyrim most go by one name, like Nazeem and Saadia. In Elder Scrolls Online many have a family name with al-, at- or af-; choose <em>Family name</em> for one, or <em>Single name</em> for the Skyrim style."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does al- mean in Redguard names?",
        "answer": "It's generally read as 'from', followed by a place, so Basim al-Taneth is Basim from Taneth. The meanings of at- and af- are less certain, but both link a person to their family."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are good female Redguard names?",
        "answer": "Set the gender to <em>Female</em> for names like Hadira and Mehrnaz. Add <em>Epithet</em> for something like Makali the Sword-Dancer."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are Redguard names Arabic?",
        "answer": "Many sound Arabic or Persian, in the games and here, and some first names here are real Arabic and Persian names. Redguards are a fictional people, so the names aren't given meanings."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I give a Redguard an epithet?",
        "answer": "Choose <em>Epithet</em> for a name with a title, like Kirzan the Hawk or a name ending <em>of the Sands</em>. It suits a sellsword or a sailor known up and down the coast."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names in Elder Scrolls Online?",
